---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';import '../../../../styles/equipment.css';const d='/equipment/14-09/',ref='https://support.flir.com/DSDownload/Assets/T810216-en-US_USL-lr.pdf';
---
<Layout pageTitle="FLIR E95 thermal camera" description="Capture a simulated reference plate and distinguish emission from reflected radiation." wide={true}><article id="e95" class="equipment"><nav class="eq-nav"><a href="/simulations/equipment/14-09/">← Equipment 14-09</a><a href="/simulations/equipment/">Register</a><a href="#e95-evidence">Science and evidence</a></nav><header class="eq-mast"><p class="eq-meta">14-09 / SURFACE THERMOGRAPHY</p><h2>One plate, two apparent temperatures</h2><p class="eq-lede">Both halves of this inert reference plate are at 60 °C. A coated patch emits most of the infrared light you see; a reflective patch returns much more of its cooler surroundings. Set the camera correction to each patch in turn. Can one setting recover both?</p></header>
<div class="e95cols"><section><h3>Inspect the E95</h3><canvas id="e95-model" aria-label="Orbitable FLIR E95 camera"></canvas><p id="e95-viewstatus" role="status">Loading camera…</p><button id="e95-front">Lens / reset</button> <button id="e95-rear">Operator view</button><p>Click the front trigger to capture. The rear touchscreen changes the palette; the center menu key switches emissivity correction. These are simplified simulator bindings, not a replica of the FLIR menu.</p></section>
<section><h3>Capture settings</h3><div class="e95form"><label>Reference target<select id="e95-mode"><option value="split">Coating ε 0.95 / metal ε 0.22</option><option value="blackbody">Uniform blackbody ε 1</option></select></label>{[['temp','True plate °C',-20,120],['env','True reflected environment °C',-20,120],['refl','Camera reflected °C',-20,120],['eps','Camera emissivity',.01,1],['dist','Distance, m',.15,2]].map(([id,label,lo,hi])=><label>{label}<input id={'e95-'+id} type="number" min={lo} max={hi} step="any"/></label>)}</div><div><button id="e95-capture">Capture plate</button> <button id="e95-match">Switch ε: coat / metal</button> <button id="e95-blackbody">40 °C blackbody example</button> <button id="e95-reset">Restore split plate</button></div></section></div>
<section><h3>Surface temperature map</h3><div class="e95out"><canvas id="e95-thermal" width="116" height="87" aria-label="Thermogram: left and right plate patches"></canvas><div><p id="e95-read" role="status"></p><p id="e95-status"></p><p>Fixed display scale: 10–75 °C, dark to bright. Hatching means outside the −20 to 120 °C measurement range; it is not a measured temperature.</p><button id="e95-palette">Iron / white hot</button> <button id="e95-json">Download map JSON</button> <button id="e95-csv">Download pixel CSV</button></div></div></section>
<section id="e95-evidence"><h3>Configuration, model and checks</h3><p>Original FLIR E95, 24° × 18° / 17 mm lens (P/N 78502-0301), not E96. <a href={ref+'#page=210'}>FLIR 2018 catalog p. 210: camera photograph and optics</a>; <a href={ref+'#page=214'}>p. 214: 278.4 × 116.1 × 113.1 mm enclosure</a>. The longest dimension runs along the grip. The 3D envelope matches those three dimensions; the grip, strap, lens, keys and connector cover are visually inferred. <a href="https://www.flir.com/products/e95/">Manufacturer specifications and photographs</a>.</p>
<div class="e95cols refs"><figure><img src="/equipment/14-09/front.webp" alt="Original front reconstruction: E95 lens, grip and trigger"/><figcaption>Original render. The lens collar, auxiliary optical windows, strap and trigger are referenced to the <a href={ref+'#page=210'}>E95 configuration photo (p. 210)</a>.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="/equipment/14-09/rear.webp" alt="Original rear reconstruction: operator display and navigation cluster"/><figcaption>Original render. The control grouping and screen are inferred from the <a href="https://pim-resources.coleparmer.com/data-sheet/39757-75thru99exx-2017elchembrochure.pdf">FLIR 2017 Exx family brochure</a>; its cover shows the related E85, not a revision-specific E95 rear drawing.</figcaption></figure></div><p>We integrate Planck radiance from 7.5 to 14 µm assuming a flat spectral response. For an opaque diffuse surface the camera receives L = εB(T) + (1−ε)B(T<sub>reflected</sub>). It subtracts the estimated reflected term, divides by assigned emissivity and inverts B. Inputs are in degrees Celsius; radiation uses kelvin. The plane fills the frame; at these short distances air transmission is treated as one. Distance changes spatial scale, not measured radiance. No tissue temperature, detector noise, calibration drift, autofocus, atmosphere, proprietary response or real instrument accuracy is simulated.</p>
<p>Hardware: 464 × 348 detector, 4-inch rear screen; this exercise renders 116 × 87 spatial bins, each representing 4 × 4 detector pixels. The export gives each map's sample ID, revision, units, radiance, true and assumed settings, pitch and top-left origin. JSON encodes the piecewise-constant map losslessly by rows; CSV expands every bin.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Independent check</th><th>Expected</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uniform 40 °C, ε = 1</td><td>40 ± 0.00001 °C in every bin</td><td>40.00000 °C; 10,092 bins</td></tr><tr><td>Independent adaptive quadrature at 60 °C</td><td>95.050912 ± 0.00001 W m⁻² sr⁻¹</td><td>95.050912 W m⁻² sr⁻¹</td></tr><tr><td>Nominal GLB width × height × depth</td><td>116.1 × 278.4 × 113.1 mm</td><td>116.1 × 278.4 × 113.1 mm</td></tr></tbody></table><p><a href={d+'build-model.py'}>Editable Blender source</a> · <a href={d+'e95.glb'}>Metric GLB</a> · <a href={d+'radiometry.mjs'}>Solver and state actions</a> · <a href={d+'vr.json'}>VR interaction bounds / budget</a> · <a href={d+'example.json'}>Example map</a>. The low-cost render has ray targets for later use in VR. No headset test or hardware measurement is claimed.</p></section></article></Layout>
<script>import '../../../../lib/equipment/14-09/client.mjs';</script>
<style is:global>#e95 .e95cols,#e95 .e95out{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem}#e95 section{padding:1rem;border:1px solid var(--border);margin:1rem 0;min-width:0}#e95 .e95form{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:.5rem}#e95 label{display:grid;gap:.2rem;min-width:0}#e95 :is(input,select,button){font:inherit;color:inherit;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--border);padding:.45rem;max-width:100%;box-sizing:border-box}#e95 button{margin:.35rem .2rem .2rem 0;cursor:pointer}#e95 button:disabled{opacity:.5}#e95 button:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #b28d5e}#e95-model{display:block;width:100%;height:320px;touch-action:none}#e95-thermal{display:block;width:100%;max-width:420px;image-rendering:pixelated;background:#25404c}#e95-read{font-size:1.25rem}#e95 figure{margin:.5rem 0}#e95 figure img{width:100%;height:auto}#e95 table{width:100%;text-align:left;font-size:.9rem}#e95 td,#e95 th{border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:.4rem}@media(max-width:760px){#e95 .e95cols,#e95 .e95out{grid-template-columns:1fr}#e95-model{height:290px}}</style>
